  "data": {
    "id": 1167842,
    "name": "Green Walnuts In Shell",
    "description": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">&nbsp;They are good for bakers who want the appearance of the full walnut- they crack out of the shell much easier and you can keep them whole or in halves and bake them to dry.</span></p><p><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong class=\"lexical-text-editor--bold\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">Not for raw snacking:</strong></b><span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"> Fresh green walnuts are extremely bitter, tannic, and astringent. They require soaking, curing, or cooking to become palatable</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong class=\"lexical-text-editor--bold\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">Liqueurs:</strong></b><span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"> They are world-famous for making </span><a href=\"https://foragerchef.com/cooking-with-green-walnuts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"lexical-text-editor--link\"><span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">Nocino</span></a><span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"> in Italy or </span><i><em class=\"lexical-text-editor--italic\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">vin de noix</em></i><span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"> in France by steeping the crushed green fruits in alcohol and sugar.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong class=\"lexical-text-editor--bold\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">Preserves &amp; Pickles:</strong></b><span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"> They are widely used to make spiced </span><a href=\"https://practicalselfreliance.com/green-walnuts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"lexical-text-editor--link\"><span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">pickled walnuts</span></a><span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">, sweet walnut jams, and traditional Greek spoon sweets (</span><i><em class=\"lexical-text-editor--italic\" style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">glyko karydaki</em></i><span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\">)</span></p>",
